
// Packing form workflow: On User Input of Sales_Order.
// Builds the Packing_Items subform: one row per line the CUSTOMER ORDERED,
// quantity = every good piece produced for that line across every QC round.
//
// WHY THIS READS Plan_Item AND NOT Quality_Check.
//
// Packing used to be built from "the" QC record. That worked while QC happened
// once. It cannot survive remakes, for three separate reasons:
//
//  1. THERE IS NO "THE" QC RECORD any more. A rejection creates remake items and
//     sends the order back to In Progress; when the remakes finish, the order
//     returns to Production Complete and a SECOND Quality_Check is raised
//     against it. Round 2 checks only the remakes.
//  2. NO SINGLE ROUND HOLDS THE ANSWER. Round 1 accepted 17 of 21 Sylph Throws;
//     round 2 accepted the 4 remade ones. Packing needs 21. Reading either
//     record alone under-packs the order.
//  3. A REMAKE IS NOT A SEPARATE THING TO PACK. It is replacement pieces for a
//     line the customer already ordered once. Concatenating both rounds' subform
//     rows gives "Sylph 17" and "Sylph 4" as two lines against an order that has
//     one - the packer is left to add them up and the box count is wrong.
//
// So the total is rolled up per ORIGINAL line. Plan_Item.Qty_Accepted is written
// by createRemakeItems on every round, cumulatively, and Remake_Of on a remake
// always points at the ROOT line - never at the previous remake - so the roll-up
// is one flat pass with no recursion (Deluge has none).
//
// This is deliberately the SAME arithmetic createRemakeItems uses to decide the
// order has passed QC. One rule, two readers: packing can never disagree with
// the status that let it be packed.
//
// FORM CHANGE: Packing.QC_No must be DELETED. It is a lookup pointing at the
// Quality_Check form, which Checking replaces - so Creator will either refuse to
// delete that form or leave this field dangling at one. Nothing reads it, and
// nothing writes it any more either: see the block further down for why the
// grain change made it unfillable rather than merely unused.
//
// DELUGE TRAPS GUARDED HERE:
//  - There is no reliable `break` in a for-each. An old version relied on one to
//    take a single record and stop; the loop ran on regardless and the subform
//    got every round's rows appended. The idiom here is "keep assigning, last
//    write wins" - which is what the break was trying to express anyway.
//  - Query results need .count(); .size() is a List method.
//  - A numeric field never written is an EMPTY STRING, not null and not 0.
//    ifnull() does not catch it and toDecimal() throws on it.
//  - A lookup holds a record id, so Item_Sku.SKU does not chain - the master
//    record has to be fetched.
//  - `sort by` will not parse inline in a for-each header.

salesId = input.Sales_Order;

input.Assigned_To = null;
input.Packing_Items.clear();

if(salesId != null)
{
	//----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// THE QC ROUND BLOCK THAT STOOD HERE IS GONE, along with the form it read.
	//
	// It gathered every Quality_Check on the order into Packing.QC_No - a multi
	// select lookup - so a packing slip named the inspections behind it.
	//
	// Checking replaced that at a different GRAIN. Quality_Check was one record
	// per order per round; Item_Check is one per BATCH. A twenty-line order that
	// went round twice produced two records before and can produce forty now, so
	// the same field would hold a list nobody can read, naming every batch
	// instead of summarising an inspection.
	//
	// Nothing is lost that this script was actually relying on. The packed
	// quantities come from Plan_Item.Qty_Accepted below, which saveItemCheck now
	// writes - so the figures still trace to an inspection, one line at a time
	// and through the item rather than through a header. Which checks covered a
	// given item is one query from the item itself: Item_Check[Plan_Item == id].
	//
	// Packing.QC_No must be DELETED in Creator. It is a lookup pointing at the
	// Quality_Check form, so - exactly like Plan_Item.QC_Ref - Creator will
	// either refuse to delete that form or leave this field dangling at one.
	//----------------------------------------------------------------------

	//----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Every plan on the order. One order produces exactly one plan today -
	// createProductionPlans inserts one inside its per-order loop - but
	// collecting them all costs nothing and mirrors what qcAutoPopulate does.
	//----------------------------------------------------------------------
	planIds = List();
	packSup = null;
	orderPlans = Production_Planning[Sales_Order == salesId] sort by Added_Time;
	for each  plan in orderPlans
	{
		planIds.add(plan.ID);

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// WHO THIS ORDER BELONGS TO, taken straight off the plan.
		//
		// This used to go the long way round: read Quality_Check.Assigned_to,
		// which qcAutoPopulate had filled with the supervisor's NAME, then
		// find the Employee again with Employee[Employee_Name == thatName].
		// Three ways to fail silently, and all of them leave the field blank
		// with no error:
		//
		//  - Assigned_to empty on QC records raised before qcAutoPopulate
		//    wrote it, or under any other link name;
		//  - the name not matching an Employee exactly - a rename, a double
		//    space, a change of case;
		//  - two employees sharing a name, where the last one read wins.
		//
		// Production_Planning.Assigned_To is ALREADY an Employee record id, so
		// there is nothing to match on and no name to get wrong. It is also
		// the same value qcAutoPopulate started from, so this is the original
		// source rather than a copy of a copy.
		//
		// FIRST plan, not last. One order produces one plan; with more it is
		// the one that started the order, which is the useful name to put on a
		// packing slip - and it is the same choice qcAutoPopulate makes.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		if(packSup == null)
		{
			packSup = plan.Assigned_To;
		}
	}

	if(packSup != null)
	{
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// PICK ONE, depending on how Assigned_To was defined on the form.
		//
		// A) Assigned_To is a LOOKUP to Employee. It takes the record id -
		//    this is the line below.
		// B) Assigned_To is a Single Line text field. Swap to the commented
		//    block, which fetches the name.
		//
		// Getting this wrong throws, and a form workflow that throws stops
		// dead - so the subform below would come up empty too. An empty
		// Assigned_To with a populated subform means this block did not run
		// at all, NOT that it ran and failed.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		input.Assigned_To = packSup;

		// for each  empA in Employee[ID == packSup]
		// {
		// 	input.Assigned_To = empA.Employee_Name;
		// }
	}

	rows = Collection();

	// SKU lookups cached across lines. A remake repeats its original's SKU and
	// the same item can legitimately appear twice on one order, so without this
	// the same Item_Master record gets fetched several times per order.
	skuCache = Map();

	for each  pid in planIds
	{
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// PASS 1 - accepted totals, keyed on the ROOT line.
		//
		// A root line keys on its own id; a remake keys on its Remake_Of. So
		// one sweep of the plan's items produces the finished-good count for
		// every customer line, remakes folded in.
		//
		// Two passes over two separate queries rather than one query iterated
		// twice - two live iterators over the same result do not work - and
		// still far cheaper than the obvious shape, which is a
		// Plan_Item[Remake_Of == root.ID] query per line.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		accByRoot = Map();

		allItems = Plan_Item[Plan == pid];
		for each  it in allItems
		{
			rootKey = it.ID.toString();
			if(it.Remake_Of != null)
			{
				rootKey = it.Remake_Of.toString();
			}

			accS = ifnull(it.Qty_Accepted,"0").toString().trim();
			if(accS == "")
			{
				accS = "0";
			}

			haveS = ifnull(accByRoot.get(rootKey),"0").toString().trim();
			if(haveS == "")
			{
				haveS = "0";
			}

			accByRoot.put(rootKey,(haveS.toDecimal().toLong() + accS.toDecimal().toLong()).toString());
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// PASS 2 - one row per ORIGINAL line, in Line_No order.
		//
		// Is_Remake is filtered in code, not in the query. The field was added
		// to a form that already had records, and a Creator field never written
		// is EMPTY rather than false - so Plan_Item[Is_Remake == false] would
		// silently drop every item planned before the field existed, and those
		// are precisely the original lines.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		rootItems = Plan_Item[Plan == pid] sort by Line_No;
		for each  ri in rootItems
		{
			if(ifnull(ri.Is_Remake,false) == false)
			{
				packQty = ifnull(accByRoot.get(ri.ID.toString()),"0").toString().trim();
				if(packQty == "")
				{
					packQty = "0";
				}

				skuKey = "";
				skuTxt = "";
				if(ri.Item_Sku != null)
				{
					skuKey = ri.Item_Sku.toString();
					skuTxt = ifnull(skuCache.get(skuKey),"").toString();
					if(skuTxt == "")
					{
						for each  im in Item_Master[ID == ri.Item_Sku]
						{
							skuTxt = ifnull(im.SKU,"").toString();
						}
						skuCache.put(skuKey,skuTxt);
					}
				}

				//----------------------------------------------------------
				// A line with nothing accepted is still listed, at 0. The
				// packing list mirrors the sales order line for line - a line
				// that silently vanished would look packed rather than short,
				// which is the one reading nobody can recover from.
				//----------------------------------------------------------
				row = Packing.Packing_Items();
				row.Item_Name=ifnull(ri.Item_Name,"").toString();
				row.SKU=skuTxt;
				row.Quantity_Packed=packQty.toDecimal().toLong();
				row.No_Of_Box=0;
				rows.insert(row);
			}
		}
	}

	input.Packing_Items.insert(rows);
}
